TASK Deprecated {}`

*Introduced/last revised: sprint 2, version 2.0.0*

### `Generate`

**Grammar:** `generation ::= "GENERATE" "(" gen_attr_list? ")" "->" IDENT ":" gen_type
gen_attr_list ::= gen_attr ("," gen_attr)*
gen_attr ::= "quantity" "=" UNSIGNED_INT | IDENT "=" value
UNSIGNED_INT ::= "0" | [1-9][0-9]*
gen_type ::= "NUMBER" | "STRING" | "BOOL" | "LIST" "[" gen_type "]"
-- A quantity attribute, if present, must use the quantity production above; quantity=NUMBER through the generic attribute production is a static error.`

**Semantics:** GENERATE is a pure, non-effectful construct that invokes a generative model to produce a value without external side effects. It shares Action's canonical attribute vocabulary and duplicate-attribute prohibition. target is always the requested output artifact, never content; content is the source instruction or text payload; audience is the intended reader; recipient is forbidden. Its optional tone and register_note follow Action's shared tone normalization rules and are mutually exclusive.

A GENERATE result type must be a gen_type: NUMBER, STRING, BOOL, or a finite nesting of LIST around those types. REF[T] is prohibited at every nesting depth: GENERATE may not declare REF[T], LIST[REF[T]], or any type containing REF. This restriction follows the global REF rule: generated text or values cannot manufacture an opaque runtime identity. The generator must return a value exactly matching its declared gen_type, including homogeneous recursively valid list elements.

quantity is an exact output-size constraint, not a maximum, minimum, estimate, or preference. Its lexical form is UNSIGNED_INT only: it may have no sign, decimal point, exponent, whitespace, or leading zero except for the literal 0. quantity is legal only for results declared STRING or LIST[T]. For LIST[T], the result contains exactly quantity elements. For STRING, it contains exactly quantity words. A word is a maximal non-empty sequence of Unicode characters whose General_Category is a Letter category (L*) or Decimal_Number (Nd); every other Unicode character is a separator. Thus quantity=0 on STRING requires a string containing no such sequence, and quantity=0 on LIST[T] requires an empty list. The executor validates the declared type and quantity condition before completion; inability to produce a conforming result fails the step under the general external-failure rule.

GENERATE must always bind its result using -> IDENT : gen_type. Use GENERATE to produce an artifact for the agent environment; use CONVO and UTTER to record observed dialogue or dialogue history.

**Example:**
- NL: "Give the user an opinion about taxes in exactly 50 words."
- BrainCode: `ENTRYPOINT TaxesOpinion

TASK TaxesOpinion : STRING {
  GENERATE(target="response", audience="user", content="Respond to the user's view that taxes are bad.", quantity=50) -> response : STRING
  RETURN response
}`

*Introduced/last revised: sprint 7, version 6.0.0*


Proposed changes available for this translation:
- [add vocabulary] structural-word (closed): The closed inventory of BrainCode keywords, operators, delimiters, and lexical forms. Members: TASK, ACTION, GENERATE, UTTER, CONVO, TURN, ENTRYPOINT, IF, THEN, ELSE, FOR, EACH, IN, ANY, ALL, SATISFIES, LET, RETURN, NUMBER, STRING, BOOL, LIST, REF, TRUE, FALSE, SPEAKER, USER, AGENT, REPLY_TO, REVISES, DECREASES, CONTEXT, AND, OR, NOT, {, }, (, ), [, ], ,, :, =, ->, ==, !=, <, <=, >, >=, -; membership rule: Lexical rules: IDENT is `[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*` excluding reserved words. NUMBER is `-? [0-9]+ ( \. [0-9]+ )? ( [eE] [+-]? [0-9]+ )?`. STRING is `"` followed by any chars except unescaped `"` and `\`, escaping with `\"`, `\\`, `\n`, `\t`, `\uXXXX`, closed by `"`. COMMENT is `#` to line end. ISO_DATE is exactly `YYYY-MM-DD`. UTC_TIMESTAMP is `YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ssZ`. Whitespace separates tokens.
- [add vocabulary] operation-vocabulary (open): The initial index of normative Action and UTTER operations. Not exhaustive; missing domains will be added. Members: open_page, click, select_filter, apply_filters, sort, search_travel, search_transit, search_web, fill_field, submit, extract, read_page, save_favorite, add_to_cart, pick_up, place, heat, chill, rinse, slice, pour, open_container, close_container, turn_on, turn_off, modify_code, run_tests, add_file, delete_file, send_email, send_message, call, check_replied, file_age_days, path_in_folder, create_list, add_to_list, check_support_status, offer_discount, offer_expedited_shipping, ask, inform, respond, propose, correct, acknowledge, confirm, decline; membership rule: Custom operations: tokenization splits on whitespace. If the source verb matches a listed synonym, use the listed operation. Otherwise, the custom operation is `custom_<domain>_<verb>`. Domain precedence: web, household, code, communication, filesystem, application, support, unknown. Select the first domain conceptually fitting the target. Verb is the lemmatized (WordNet base form), lowercase, underscore-joined head verb (e.g., 'set up' -> 'set_up'). Collision handling: if two distinct actions yield the same `custom_` name, append `_2` to the second in source order. Fallback domain is `unknown`.
- [add vocabulary] attribute-name (open): The initial index of attributes mapping each to its permitted value category or literal rule. Members: target, destination, source, relation, destination_location, destination_relation, content, recipient, audience, audience_role, tone, register_note, format, style, locale, quantity, duration, duration_unit, execution_date, origin, date, return_date, passengers, near, location, distance, min_rating, max_price, currency, genre, category, color, size, shape, availability, audience_class, rank_field, rank_direction, ordering, limit, deadline, metric, discount_percent, module, class, function, call, argument, assertion, reproduction_step, expected_output, actual_output, environment, platform, test_id, exclude, instrument; membership rule: Value rules: `tone` uses `tone-value`. `format`, `style`, `locale`, `duration_unit` use `artifact-specification`. `currency`, `genre`, `category`, `availability`, `rank_field`, `rank_direction` use `search-value`. `relation`, `destination_relation` use `spatial-relation`. `recipient`, `audience_role` use `role-recipient`. `target`, `destination`, `source`, `instrument` use a bound REF, an `entity-name` member, or a transitional STRING if no member applies. Code identifiers (`module`, `class`, `function`, `call`, `argument`, `assertion`, `test_id`) use a STRING literal matching the exact identifier. `expected_output`, `actual_output`, `reproduction_step`, `environment`, `platform`, `content`, `exclude`, `register_note`, `audience` remain transitional STRING payloads until further categories are added. Numeric fields use NUMBER. Dates use STRING matching ISO_DATE or UTC_TIMESTAMP.
- [add vocabulary] entity-name (open): Canonical entity and location base nouns. Members: mug, rag, tub, book, mirror, sink, counter, cupboard, refrigerator, spoon, bowl, pillow, sofa, armchair, toilet_paper, holder, wall, motherboard, processor, decorative_led_candles, cup, coffee_maker, microwave, toilet; membership rule: An entity or location symbol must be a singular, lowercase_underscore noun phrase representing the physical or conceptual object. It must not contain verbs, states, or modifiers. If it appears in the source, it must be extracted as the head noun phrase. This category is distinct from unrestricted IDENTs which are used for variables and handles.
- [add vocabulary] spatial-relation (closed): The closed set of relations used when placing one entity relative to another. Members: in, on, under, next_to, left_of, right_of, in_front_of, behind
- [add vocabulary] search-value (open): Canonical namespaced values for search, filtering, ranking, currency, genre, category, and availability attributes. Members: rank_price, rank_rating, rank_distance, dir_asc, dir_desc, curr_usd, cat_game, cat_limited_time_offers, avail_in_stock, avail_out_of_stock; membership rule: Members are prefixed by their facet: `rank_` for rank_field, `dir_` for rank_direction, `curr_` for currency, `genre_` for genre, `cat_` for category, `avail_` for availability. An attribute must only accept symbols with its corresponding prefix.
- [add vocabulary] role-recipient (open): Canonical social or organizational roles used as recipients and audiences. Members: user, agent, manager, customer, professor, support_agent; membership rule: A lowercase_underscore relationship or institutional role.
- [add vocabulary] tone-value (closed): The closed set of canonical tone values used by communication, utterance, and generation attributes. Members: formal, polite, neutral, urgent, casual, friendly, empathetic, firm, apologetic, persuasive, humorous, supportive, concise, detailed; membership rule: When a symbol (e.g., detailed, concise) exists in multiple categories, it is resolved by the expected category of the attribute it binds to.
- [add vocabulary] artifact-specification (open): Canonical artifact targets and generation facets for format, style, locale, and duration units. Members: story, short_text, technical_introduction, contract, structured_report, character_profile, scene, plan, bullet_points, structured_sections, paragraph, balanced, detailed, concise, en_us, day, hour, week; membership rule: Each member belongs to exactly one facet: artifact target, format, style, locale, or duration unit. When a symbol (e.g., detailed) exists in multiple categories, it is resolved by the attribute's expected category.
- [revise] Action: grammar `action ::= "ACTION" operation-id "(" action-attrs? ")" ("->" IDENT ":" type)?
action-attrs ::= action-attr ("," action-attr)*
action-attr ::= attribute-name "=" value
operation-id ::= operation-vocabulary member

-- Signatures and Result Categories (SAME, NEW, MEMBER, VALUE, NONE):
-- open_page(target) -> NONE
-- click(target) -> NONE
-- select_filter(target, content) -> LIST[REF[T]] [SAME]
-- apply_filters(target, content) -> LIST[REF[T]] [SAME]
-- sort(target, rank_field, rank_direction) -> LIST[REF[T]] [SAME]
-- search_travel(origin, destination, date, [passengers, return_date]) -> LIST[REF[STRING]] [NEW]
-- search_transit(origin, destination, [date, near]) -> LIST[REF[STRING]] [NEW]
-- search_web(target, [audience_class, availability, category, color, currency, distance, genre, location, max_price, min_rating, shape, size]) -> LIST[REF[STRING]] [NEW]
-- fill_field(target, content) -> NONE
-- submit(target) -> NONE
-- extract(target, limit) -> REF[T] or LIST[REF[T]] [MEMBER]
-- read_page(target, [content]) -> STRING [VALUE]
-- save_favorite(target) -> NONE
-- add_to_cart(target) -> NONE
-- pick_up(target, [source]) -> REF[STRING] [SAME]
-- place(target, destination, [destination_location, destination_relation, relation]) -> NONE
-- heat(target, [destination]) -> REF[STRING] [SAME]
-- chill(target, [destination]) -> REF[STRING] [SAME]
-- rinse(target, destination) -> REF[STRING] [SAME]
-- slice(target) -> LIST[REF[STRING]] [NEW]
-- pour(target, destination) -> NONE
-- open_container(target) -> NONE
-- close_container(target) -> NONE
-- turn_on(target) -> NONE
-- turn_off(target) -> NONE
-- modify_code(target, [actual_output, argument, assertion, call, class, content, environment, expected_output, function, module, platform, reproduction_step]) -> NONE
-- run_tests(target, [test_id]) -> NONE
-- add_file(target, content) -> NONE
-- delete_file(target) -> NONE
-- send_email(recipient, content, [tone]) -> NONE
-- send_message(recipient, content, [tone]) -> NONE
-- call(recipient) -> NONE
-- check_replied(target, deadline) -> BOOL [VALUE]
-- file_age_days(target) -> NUMBER [VALUE]
-- path_in_folder(target, destination) -> BOOL [VALUE]
-- create_list(target) -> REF[STRING] [NEW]
-- add_to_list(target, destination) -> NONE
-- check_support_status(target, metric) -> BOOL [VALUE]
-- offer_discount(target, [content, discount_percent]) -> NONE
-- offer_expedited_shipping(target, [content]) -> NONE
-- custom_<domain>_<verb>(target, [content, destination, instrument]) -> NONE` — An Action attribute is valid only if it is an attribute-name member, its value conforms to the attribute's declared category/rule, and it is listed in that operation's signature. Canonical ordering rule: Attributes must be emitted in exactly their declared signature order (required first, then optional), with any optional attributes present sorted alphabetically by attribute name. When the source text does not impose a sequential order for independent steps, emit them in alphabetical order of their operations, breaking ties by target. Dedicated search attributes are canonical: a criterion represented by a dedicated attribute must not also appear in select_filter/apply_filters content. Handle naming rule: A bound REF IDENT must be the normalized singular head noun of the entity; a LIST[REF[T]] must be the plural. If a collision occurs, append a numeric suffix `_2`, `_3` in source order. Existing coreference behavior remains unchanged.
