TASK Deprecated {}`

*Introduced/last revised: sprint 2, version 2.0.0*

### `Generate`

**Grammar:** `generation ::= "GENERATE" "(" generate-attr ("," generate-attr)* ")" "->" IDENT ":" gen_type
generate-attr ::= attribute-name "=" value
gen_type ::= "NUMBER" | "STRING" | "BOOL" | "LIST" "[" gen_type"]"`

**Semantics:** Only target, audience, content, duration, duration_unit, format, locale, quantity, style, and tone are legal Generate attributes, validated against their attribute-name rules. Canonical ordering rule: Attributes must be emitted in exactly their declared signature order (target first, followed by optional attributes sorted alphabetically). Handle rule: The bound IDENT must reflect the target artifact's normalized noun. If a collision occurs, append a numeric suffix `_2`, `_3` in source order. Quoted entity strings are a transitional exception; entity-name symbols must be used whenever a member exists. content remains a transitional STRING channel.

**Example:**
- NL: "Help me to create a text about my decision to go to Japan to learn about SGI. Only 100 words."
- BrainCode: `ENTRYPOINT WriteJapanSGIText
TASK WriteJapanSGIText : STRING {
  GENERATE(target=art_short_text, content="Write about my decision to go to Japan to learn about SGI", quantity=100) -> short_text : STRING
  RETURN short_text
}`

*Introduced/last revised: sprint 17, version 9.0.0*


Glossary:
## Vocabulary

The descriptive lexicon: every category of symbol an expression may use besides the constructs above (actions, objects, roles, attributes and their allowed values, literal forms). Closed categories list every member; open categories list representative members plus the rule that defines membership.

### `structural-word` (vocabulary)

**Gloss:** The closed inventory of BrainCode keywords, operators, delimiters, and lexical forms.

**Category:** closed — the members below are exhaustive

**Members:**
- `TASK` — Declares a task.
- `ACTION` — Executes an external operation.
- `GENERATE` — Generates a non-effectful artifact.
- `UTTER` — Records a speech act.
- `CONVO` — Declares a conversation.
- `TURN` — Declares a conversation turn.
- `ENTRYPOINT` — Names the single execution root.
- `IF` — Starts a conditional branch.
- `THEN` — Starts the true branch.
- `ELSE` — Starts the false branch.
- `FOR` — Starts iteration.
- `EACH` — Marks per-member iteration.
- `IN` — Introduces an iterable or relation.
- `ANY` — Existential quantifier.
- `ALL` — Universal quantifier.
- `SATISFIES` — Introduces a quantifier condition.
- `LET` — Binds a value.
- `RETURN` — Returns a task value.
- `NUMBER` — Numeric type.
- `STRING` — Text type.
- `BOOL` — Boolean type.
- `LIST` — List type.
- `REF` — Runtime entity-reference type.
- `TRUE` — Boolean true literal.
- `FALSE` — Boolean false literal.
- `SPEAKER` — Speaker label.
- `USER` — User speaker.
- `AGENT` — Agent speaker.
- `REPLY_TO` — Turn adjacency.
- `REVISES` — Turn correction.
- `DECREASES` — Recursion guard.
- `CONTEXT` — Task context.
- `AND` — Logical AND.
- `OR` — Logical OR.
- `NOT` — Logical NOT.
- `{` — Block start.
- `}` — Block end.
- `(` — Paren start.
- `)` — Paren end.
- `[` — Bracket start.
- `]` — Bracket end.
- `,` — Separator.
- `:` — Type annotator.
- `=` — Assignment.
- `->` — Result bind.
- `==` — Equality.
- `!=` — Inequality.
- `<` — Less-than.
- `<=` — Less-or-equal.
- `>` — Greater-than.
- `>=` — Greater-or-equal.
- `-` — Arithmetic difference.

**Membership rule:** Lexical rules: IDENT is `[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*` excluding reserved words. NUMBER is `-? [0-9]+ ( \. [0-9]+ )? ( [eE] [+-]? [0-9]+ )?`. STRING is `"` followed by any chars except unescaped `"` and `\`, escaping with `\"`, `\\`, `\n`, `\t`, `\uXXXX`, closed by `"`. COMMENT is `#` to line end. ISO_DATE is exactly `YYYY-MM-DD`. UTC_TIMESTAMP is `YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ssZ`. Whitespace separates tokens. Every retained lexical punctuation/form used by the full grammar is either a structural-word member or covered by these named lexical rules.

**Example:**
- NL: "Return the number 5."
- BrainCode: `ENTRYPOINT SimpleTask
TASK SimpleTask : NUMBER {
  LET five : NUMBER = 5
  RETURN five
}`

**Introduced/last revised:** sprint 17, see `changelog.md#sprint-17`

### `operation-vocabulary` (vocabulary)

**Gloss:** The initial index of normative Action and UTTER operations. Missing domains use a deterministic custom rule.

**Category:** open — the members below are representative

**Members:**
- `open_page` — Navigate to a page. (natural-language synonyms: go to)
- `click` — Click an element.
- `select_filter` — Apply one filter.
- `apply_filters` — Apply multiple filters.
- `sort` — Rank a list. (natural-language synonyms: rank, order by)
- `search_travel` — Search travel.
- `search_transit` — Search transit.
- `search_web` — Search the web. (natural-language synonyms: find, look for)
- `fill_field` — Fill a form field.
- `submit` — Submit a form.
- `extract` — Select members of a list. (natural-language synonyms: select)
- `read_page` — Read page content.
- `save_favorite` — Save to favorites.
- `add_to_cart` — Add to cart.
- `pick_up` — Acquire an object. (natural-language synonyms: grab, take)
- `place` — Put an object. (natural-language synonyms: put)
- `heat` — Heat an object.
- `chill` — Chill an object.
- `rinse` — Wash an object. (natural-language synonyms: wash)
- `slice` — Cut into pieces. (natural-language synonyms: chop, cut)
- `pour` — Pour a liquid.
- `open_container` — Open a container.
- `close_container` — Close a container.
- `turn_on` — Turn on a device.
- `turn_off` — Turn off a device.
- `modify_code` — Change code. (natural-language synonyms: fix)
- `run_tests` — Run tests.
- `add_file` — Add a file.
- `delete_file` — Delete a file.
- `send_email` — Send email. (natural-language synonyms: email)
- `send_message` — Send message. (natural-language synonyms: text)
- `call` — Make a call.
- `check_replied` — Check for reply.
- `file_age_days` — Get file age.
- `path_in_folder` — Check path.
- `create_list` — Create a list.
- `add_to_list` — Add to list.
- `check_support_status` — Query support.
- `offer_discount` — Offer discount.
- `offer_expedited_shipping` — Offer shipping.
- `ask` — Ask a question.
- `inform` — State information.
- `respond` — Answer.
- `propose` — Propose an idea.
- `correct` — Correct a statement.
- `acknowledge` — Acknowledge.
- `confirm` — Confirm.
- `decline` — Decline.

**Membership rule:** A self-contained deterministic mapping procedure: operations are strictly limited to the enumerated members. If a domain-specific operation is required but missing, it uses the atomic token rule `custom_<domain>_<action>` where `<domain>` and `<action>` are exactly `[a-z_]+` strings. No external lemmatizer or English dictionary is used; the symbol must be an exact atomic token.

**Example:**
- NL: "Examine a book."
- BrainCode: `ACTION custom_household_examine(target=book, content="examine")`

**Introduced/last revised:** sprint 17, see `changelog.md#sprint-17`

### `attribute-name` (vocabulary)

**Gloss:** The initial index of attributes mapping each to its permitted value category or literal rule.

**Category:** open — the members below are representative

**Members:**
- `target` — Subject/artifact.
- `destination` — Destination.
- `source` — Source.
- `relation` — Spatial relation.
- `content` — Transitional payload.
- `recipient` — Recipient.
- `audience` — Named reader.
- `tone` — Register.
- `format` — Artifact format.
- `style` — Artifact style.
- `locale` — Artifact locale.
- `quantity` — Exact count.
- `duration` — Numeric duration.
- `duration_unit` — Duration unit.
- `execution_date` — TASK context anchor.
- `origin` — Travel origin.
- `date` — Travel date.
- `return_date` — Travel return.
- `passengers` — Passenger count.
- `location` — Search/placement location.
- `distance` — Search distance.
- `min_rating` — Minimum rating.
- `max_price` — Maximum price.
- `currency` — Price currency.
- `genre` — Search genre.
- `category` — Search category.
- `availability` — Search stock.
- `rank_field` — Sort field.
- `rank_direction` — Sort direction.
- `limit` — Extract member count.
- `deadline` — Reply deadline.
- `metric` — Support metric.
- `platform` — Platform constraint.
- `modifier` — Entity modifier.
- `state` — Entity state.
- `itinerary` — Travel itinerary.
- `route` — Travel route.
- `travel_time` — Travel time.
- `stop_count` — Transit stops.
- `policy` — Policy.
- `amenity` — Amenity.
- `venue` — Venue.
- `showtime` — Showtime.
- `ticket` — Ticket.
- `seat` — Seat.
- `compatibility` — Compatibility.
- `file` — Code file.
- `method` — Code method.
- `property` — Code property.
- `call` — Code call.
- `assertion` — Code assertion.
- `expected_output` — Expected behavior.
- `actual_output` — Observed behavior.
- `reproduction_step` — Bug reproduction.
- `revision` — Revision.
- `procedure` — Procedure.
- `safety` — Safety.
- `escalation` — Escalation.
- `academic_goal` — Academic goal.
- `job_search_goal` — Job search goal.
- `food` — Food.
- `nutrition` — Nutrition.
- `meal` — Meal structure.
- `medical_topic` — Medical topic.
- `medical_audience` — Medical audience.
- `message_structure` — Message structure.
- `collaboration_structure` — Collaboration structure.

**Membership rule:** Maps attributes to their permitted value categories. `platform` uses `platform-name`. `location`, `destination` use `location-name` or `entity-name`. `relation` uses `spatial-relation`. Search facets use `search-value`. Descriptive domain attributes (roles, tones, artifacts, modifiers, states, travel, code, medical) use `descriptive-value`. `target` uses `entity-name`. Quoted entity strings are a transitional exception; entity-name symbols must be used whenever a member exists.

**Example:**
- NL: "Search comedy tv shows on netflix."
- BrainCode: `ACTION search_web(target=tv_show, genre=genre_comedy, platform=netflix) -> tv_shows : LIST[REF[STRING]]`

**Introduced/last revised:** sprint 17, see `changelog.md#sprint-17`

### `entity-name` (vocabulary)

**Gloss:** Canonical entity base nouns.

**Category:** open — the members below are representative

**Members:**
- `mug` — Drinking cup.
- `rag` — Cleaning cloth.
- `tub` — Bathtub.
- `book` — Reading material.
- `mirror` — Reflective item.
- `sink` — Washing basin.
- `counter` — Countertop surface.
- `cupboard` — Storage cabinet.
- `refrigerator` — Cooling appliance. (natural-language synonyms: fridge)
- `spoon` — Eating utensil.
- `bowl` — A bowl.
- `pillow` — A cushion.
- `sofa` — A couch. (natural-language synonyms: couch)
- `armchair` — A chair with armrests.
- `toilet_paper` — Toilet paper.
- `holder` — A holder.
- `wall` — A wall.
- `motherboard` — Computer motherboard.
- `processor` — Computer processor.
- `decorative_led_candles` — LED candles.
- `cup` — A cup.
- `coffee_maker` — Coffee machine.
- `microwave` — Microwave oven.
- `toilet` — A toilet.
- `apple` — An apple.
- `tv_show` — Television program.
- `dog_collar` — Collar for a dog.

**Membership rule:** A self-contained atomic-symbol rule: an entity-name is an exact symbol matching `[a-z_]+` representing a discrete entity class. It does not rely on unspecified English head-noun extraction. If a member exists, it must be used instead of a quoted string.

**Example:**
- NL: "Pick up the rag."
- BrainCode: `ACTION pick_up(target=rag) -> rag : REF[STRING]`

**Introduced/last revised:** sprint 17, see `changelog.md#sprint-17`

### `search-value` (vocabulary)

**Gloss:** Canonical namespaced values for search, filtering, ranking, currency, genre, category, and availability attributes.

**Category:** open — the members below are representative

**Members:**
- `rank_price` — Rank or filter field: monetary cost. (natural-language synonyms: cheapest, lowest price)
- `rank_rating` — Rank or filter field: user or critic score.
- `rank_distance` — Rank field: proximity. (natural-language synonyms: nearest, closest)
- `dir_asc` — Ascending rank direction. (natural-language synonyms: lowest first)
- `dir_desc` — Descending rank direction. (natural-language synonyms: highest first)
- `curr_usd` — United States dollar currency. (natural-language synonyms: $)
- `cat_game` — Category: video game.
- `cat_limited_time_offers` — Category: time-limited deals.
- `avail_in_stock` — Available for purchase now. (natural-language synonyms: available)
- `avail_out_of_stock` — Unavailable for purchase now. (natural-language synonyms: sold out)
- `genre_comedy` — Genre: comedy.

**Membership rule:** Each open facet is usable deterministically: members are prefixed by `rank_`, `dir_`, `curr_`, `genre_`, `cat_`, `avail_`. The suffix must be a self-contained atomic `[a-z_]+` string (e.g., `genre_comedy`). Translators map to known symbols or emit the atomic rule directly.

**Example:**
- NL: "Find the cheapest mirror under $20."
- BrainCode: `ACTION search_web(target=mirror, currency=curr_usd, max_price=20) -> mirrors : LIST[REF[STRING]]
ACTION sort(target=mirrors, rank_field=rank_price, rank_direction=dir_asc) -> sorted_mirrors : LIST[REF[STRING]]`

**Introduced/last revised:** sprint 17, see `changelog.md#sprint-17`

### `spatial-relation` (vocabulary)

**Gloss:** The closed set of relations used when placing one entity relative to another.

**Category:** closed — the members below are exhaustive

**Members:**
- `in` — Contained within. (natural-language synonyms: inside)
- `on` — Resting atop. (natural-language synonyms: on top of)
- `under` — Beneath. (natural-language synonyms: below)
- `next_to` — Adjacent to. (natural-language synonyms: beside)
- `left_of` — To the left of. (natural-language synonyms: left of)
- `right_of` — To the right of. (natural-language synonyms: right of)
- `in_front_of` — Positioned in front of. (natural-language synonyms: in front of)
- `behind` — Positioned behind.

**Example:**
- NL: "Put the bowl right of the spoon."
- BrainCode: `ACTION pick_up(target=bowl) -> bowl : REF[STRING]
ACTION pick_up(target=spoon) -> spoon : REF[STRING]
ACTION place(target=bowl, destination=spoon, relation=right_of)`

**Introduced/last revised:** sprint 17, see `changelog.md#sprint-17`

### `platform-name` (vocabulary)

**Gloss:** Canonical platform, brand, and software environment names.

**Category:** open — the members below are representative

**Members:**
- `netflix` — Streaming platform.
- `django` — Web framework.
- `sklearn` — Machine learning library.
- `youtube` — Video platform.

**Membership rule:** An atomic symbol matching `[a-z_]+` representing a software platform, service, or brand.

**Example:**
- NL: "Find comedy tv shows on netflix."
- BrainCode: `ACTION search_web(target=tv_show, genre=genre_comedy, platform=netflix) -> tv_shows : LIST[REF[STRING]]`

**Introduced/last revised:** sprint 17, see `changelog.md#sprint-17`

### `location-name` (vocabulary)

**Gloss:** Canonical physical and conceptual locations, positions, and areas.

**Category:** open — the members below are representative

**Members:**
- `corner` — A corner area.
- `table` — A table surface.
- `eastern_cape` — Eastern Cape region.
- `japan` — Country of Japan.

**Membership rule:** An atomic symbol matching `[a-z_]+` representing a physical or conceptual location, position, or area.

**Example:**
- NL: "Place it on the table in the corner."
- BrainCode: `ACTION place(target=apple_slice, destination=table, location=corner, relation=on)`

**Introduced/last revised:** sprint 17, see `changelog.md#sprint-17`

### `descriptive-value` (vocabulary)

**Gloss:** A unified category for roles, tones, artifacts, modifiers, states, and domain concepts.

**Category:** open — the members below are representative

**Members:**
- `role_user` — The requesting human.
- `role_agent` — The assistant.
- `role_manager` — The user's manager.
- `tone_polite` — Courteous.
- `tone_casual` — Informal.
- `tone_concise` — Brief.
- `art_story` — Narrative story.
- `art_short_text` — Short text.
- `art_plan` — Actionable plan.
- `art_structured_report` — Headed report.
- `mod_mixed_case` — Mixed case modifier.
- `state_microwaved` — Microwaved state.
- `dom_safari` — Safari concept.
- `dom_pain` — Pain concept.
- `dom_ovr` — One-vs-rest concept.
- `dom_sgi` — SGI concept.
- `dom_day` — Day unit.

**Membership rule:** A unified category for roles, tones, artifacts, modifiers, states, and domain concepts. Members are prefixed: `role_`, `tone_`, `art_`, `mod_`, `state_`, `dom_`. The suffix is an atomic `[a-z_]+` string. Added to satisfy the need for descriptive categories within the 12-change limit.

**Example:**
- NL: "Plan a 3 day safari."
- BrainCode: `GENERATE(target=art_plan, content="Plan a safari", duration=3, duration_unit=dom_day) -> plan : STRING`

**Introduced/last revised:** sprint 17, see `changelog.md#sprint-17`

### `format-value` (vocabulary)

**Gloss:** Canonical output-format symbols for the format attribute (used by GENERATE and message-producing Actions/Utterances).

**Category:** open — the members below are representative

**Members:**
- `format_plain_text` — Unstructured prose text. (natural-language synonyms: plain text)
- `format_bullet_list` — Bulleted list. (natural-language synonyms: bullet points, bulleted)
- `format_numbered_list` — Numbered list. (natural-language synonyms: numbered steps)
- `format_email` — Email message layout. (natural-language synonyms: as an email)
- `format_markdown` — Markdown-formatted text. (natural-language synonyms: in markdown)
- `format_table` — Tabular layout. (natural-language synonyms: as a table)
- `format_pdf` — PDF document. (natural-language synonyms: as a pdf)

**Membership rule:** An atomic symbol matching `format_[a-z_]+` naming a concrete output layout. New layouts are added as new members, never as free text.

**Example:**
- NL: "Give me the steps as a numbered list."
- BrainCode: `GENERATE(target=art_plan, content="steps", format=format_numbered_list) -> plan : STRING`

**Introduced/last revised:** sprint 18, see `changelog.md#sprint-18`


Proposed changes available for this translation:
- [revise] Generate: grammar `generation ::= "GENERATE" "(" generate-attr ("," generate-attr)* ")" "->" IDENT ":" gen_type
generate-attr ::= attribute-name "=" value
gen_type ::= "NUMBER" | "STRING" | "BOOL" | "LIST" "[" gen_type"]"` — Only target, audience, content, duration, duration_unit, format, locale, quantity, style, and tone are legal Generate attributes. target's value is an artifact-value symbol (GENERATE always produces an artifact, never an entity, so target is governed by artifact-value, distinct from Action's entity-name-governed target). audience is governed by recipient-value. tone is governed by tone-value. format is governed by format-value. duration_unit is governed by duration-unit-value. duration and quantity are NUMBER. style and locale remain governed by descriptive-value. Canonical ordering rule: attributes emitted in exactly declared signature order (target first, then optional attributes alphabetically). Handle rule: bound IDENT reflects the target artifact's normalized noun; collisions get `_2`, `_3` in source order. content remains a transitional STRING channel pending N4's closing phase.
- [revise] Flow-If: grammar `flow_if ::= "IF" check "THEN" "{" step+ "}" ( "ELSE" "{" step+ "}" )?` — Conditional branching, used identically inside TASK and CONVO TURN bodies. The Check is evaluated first, at the moment control reaches the IF. If true, THEN's steps execute in order; if false and ELSE is present, its steps execute instead; if false with no ELSE, control passes to the following step. Flow-If may nest inside another Flow-If or a FOR-EACH body; nested Checks evaluate only when control actually reaches them. THEN and ELSE each open a fresh child scope: a binding inside THEN is invisible in ELSE, after the IF, or to siblings, and vice versa. An IF nested inside a TURN body may contain UTTER in its branches exactly as it may contain ACTION; the same nesting inside a TASK body remains a static error per Utterance's restriction. When a Check depends on a preceding `check_replied` with a future `deadline`, the branch inherits that deferred timing.
- [add vocabulary] tone-value (closed): The closed set of register/tone symbols usable wherever an attribute calls for a tone (Action's tone, UTTER's tone, GENERATE's tone). Members: tone_polite, tone_formal, tone_casual, tone_concise, tone_neutral, tone_urgent, tone_empathetic, tone_professional
- [add vocabulary] duration-unit-value (closed): The closed set of time units usable with the duration_unit attribute. Members: unit_second, unit_minute, unit_hour, unit_day, unit_week, unit_month, unit_year
- [add vocabulary] currency-value (open): Canonical currency-code symbols for the currency attribute, used with price bounds in search and generation. Members: curr_usd, curr_eur, curr_gbp, curr_jpy, curr_ils; membership rule: An atomic symbol matching `curr_[a-z]{3}` naming an ISO-4217-style currency code. This is now the sole canonical location for currency symbols; search-value no longer carries curr_ members.
- [add vocabulary] recipient-value (open): Canonical role/recipient symbols for the recipient and audience attributes. Members: role_user, role_agent, role_manager, role_professor, role_friend, role_colleague, role_support_team, role_customer; membership rule: An atomic symbol matching `role_[a-z_]+` naming a person-role, class, or audience type. For a specific named individual not covered by any role symbol (e.g. a personal name like 'John'), a quoted STRING remains a transitional exception — exactly as entity-name allows for physical objects — pending a dedicated proper-name literal category under N5; a role_ symbol must be used instead whenever one applies. This preserves existing coverage of named recipients rather than making them inexpressible.
- [add vocabulary] artifact-value (open): The canonical set of generated-artifact-type symbols usable as GENERATE's target attribute. Members: art_story, art_short_text, art_plan, art_structured_report, art_technical_explanation, art_character_profile; membership rule: An atomic symbol matching `art_[a-z_]+` naming a class of generated artifact (document type, narrative form, structured output). Per N6, assume the complete glossary already contains the needed artifact member for a given request; a genuinely new artifact type is added as a new member in a future sprint, never expressed as free text.
- [revise vocabulary] descriptive-value (open): A residual, still-open category for entity modifiers, entity states, and unclassified domain concepts not yet covered by a dedicated attribute-value category (roles, tones, currencies, artifact types, and durations now have their own categories and must not be duplicated here). Members: mod_mixed_case, state_microwaved, dom_safari, dom_pain, dom_ovr, dom_sgi; membership rule: A residual bucket for entity-modifier (`mod_`), entity-state (`state_`), and unclassified domain-concept (`dom_`) symbols. role_, tone_, curr_, art_, and unit_ symbols are retired from this category — use recipient-value, tone-value, currency-value, artifact-value, and duration-unit-value respectively instead; dom_day is retired in favor of unit_day.
- [revise vocabulary] search-value (open): Canonical namespaced values for ranking, sort-direction, genre, category, and availability attributes (currency now lives exclusively in currency-value). Members: rank_price, rank_rating, rank_distance, dir_asc, dir_desc, cat_game, cat_limited_time_offers, avail_in_stock, avail_out_of_stock, genre_comedy; membership rule: Members are prefixed by `rank_`, `dir_`, `genre_`, `cat_`, `avail_`, each an atomic `[a-z_]+` suffix. Currency no longer belongs here; use currency-value.
- [revise vocabulary] format-value (open): Canonical output-format symbols for the format attribute (used by GENERATE and message-producing Actions/Utterances). Members: format_plain_text, format_bullet_list, format_numbered_list, format_email, format_markdown, format_table, format_pdf; membership rule: A translator must use an existing member whenever one fits the requested layout. Only when no member fits may a translator mint a new atomic `format_[a-z_]+` symbol following this pattern (per N6, assume the complete glossary already lists the needed layout); that new symbol is then recorded as a glossary member in the next sprint. No free-text format description is ever permitted, regardless of whether a member currently exists.
- [add vocabulary] product-attribute-value (open): Canonical color, size, and gender/demographic-audience symbols for product search and filtering attributes. Members: color_yellow, color_red, color_black, color_white, size_small, size_medium, size_large, gender_women, gender_men, gender_unisex; membership rule: An atomic symbol matching `(color|size|gender)_[a-z_]+` naming a product color, garment/item size, or demographic-audience facet. New values are added as new members following this prefix pattern, never as free text.
